+  "title": "GOV-0108: DISCOVERED -- expected_surfaces is an optional enforcement key, so C3 containment and the C7 delegation ceiling read an input 2 of 95 work packages have ever supplied",
+  "problem": "\ud83e\udde0 **ARCHI (BARCHI-3)** \u2014 DISCOVERED: `expected_surfaces` is an **optional** enforcement key, so the C3 containment and C7 delegation controls read an input that two work packages in this repository's history have ever supplied.\n\n**Discovered-by:** WP-2026-0115 / WP-2026-0114 (measured while checking whether my own WPs declared it \u2014 they do not). Filed separately per *Discovery \u2260 scope expansion*; not fixed in either branch.\n**Measurement credit:** BARCHI-1 (`bst-sa-40`), who measured it and declined to own the issue. Independently re-measured here before filing, per the rule that a peer's claim is unmeasured until you run it.\n\n## Measured on `main` (`87dd192`)\n\n```\ntotal work packages                             95\nwith a usable expected_surfaces.files list       2   (WP-2026-0109, WP-2026-0111)\nwith expected_surfaces in some other shape       0\nwith no expected_surfaces at all                93\n```\n\nSchema: `expected_surfaces` is **not** in `work-package.schema.json`'s `required[]`. Its defined sub-keys are `files`, `services`, `interfaces`, `discovery_allowance`. There is **no `paths` key** \u2014 a point that matters below.\n\nWhere the gate reads it:\n\n```\nbadf_gate.py:1610   surface = [... (wp.get(\"expected_surfaces\") or {}).get(\"files\") or []]\nbadf_gate.py:1616   delegation declared but the work package declares no expected_surfaces (BLD-I10 / C7)\nbadf_gate.py:1666   PASS claimed with changed paths outside expected_surfaces ... (BLD-I04 / C3)\nbadf_gate.py:3539   non_coverage: \"expected_surfaces not declared ...; surface containment is not measurable\"\n```\n\n## The honest framing, which is narrower than the arithmetic\n\n**93 of 95 is not 93 authors ignoring a control.** The key was introduced late, by `WP-2026-0092` (#180), so most of those records predate its existence. BARCHI-1 caught themselves before sending the 97% version, and that caveat is the reason this issue is filed as a design question rather than an adoption scandal.\n\nThe defensible statement: **the C3 containment control and the C7 delegation ceiling read a key that two records have ever supplied.** `badf_gate.py:3539` is honest about the consequence \u2014 it emits non-coverage saying containment \"is not measurable\" \u2014 so the system is not lying about it. It is just off, by default, almost everywhere.\n\n## Why it is worth filing now rather than later\n\n`bst-sa-90` is building two-sided containment (WP-2026-0116) on top of this key: a control that refuses a **declared-but-unmatched** surface. Where nothing is declared, *both* directions are vacuous \u2014 so the strengthened control inherits the same near-empty input. Worth knowing while it is still being built.\n\n## The reframe, which I think is the actual issue\n\nNot *\"why do records omit it\"* but **whether an optional enforcement key is a coherent design.** A control that activates only when an author volunteers its input is one an author can always switch off by silence. That is the *authority* form of the vacuity class this repo has been cataloguing all week, rather than the tooling form: not a guard that passes when its input is missing by accident, but a guard whose input is missing by permission.\n\nTwo live illustrations that it is not hypothetical:\n\n1. **BARCHI-1 declared `paths` instead of `files`.** The schema defines no `paths`, the gate reads `files`, and `work-package.schema.json` is permissive \u2014 so the declaration validated, passed `repo`, and enforced **nothing**. A scope commitment that is silently inert looks identical to one that works. Caught pre-push only because another seat happened to use the correct key in an unrelated message.\n2. **My own two WPs declare nothing at all** \u2014 which is not the same as passing. The `files`/`paths` trap cannot fire on me, but not because I got the key right: because I made no machine-checkable scope commitment for the control to read.\n\n## Relationship to open issues\n\n- **#240** (schema objects are permissive; an invented key validates and is silently ignored) is the mechanism behind illustration 1 and is the closer fix for it. This issue is adjacent, not duplicate: #240 is about *wrong* keys validating; this is about a *correct* key being optional.\n- **#232** (GOV-0102: C3 containment is one-sided; an over-broad `expected_surfaces` silently widens the C7 ceiling) is the same key from the opposite direction \u2014 over-broad declaration vs. absent declaration. The two should probably be resolved together.\n\n## Not proposing a fix\n\nDeliberately. The options \u2014 make it required, derive it from the diff, keep it optional but make the non-coverage blocking rather than advisory, or fold it into #240's separator so the expectation becomes a fact in the schema \u2014 trade differently against 93 existing records and against the Lean gate, and that is an architecture call rather than an engineering one. Acceptance criteria and a version tier belong in whichever plan takes it.\n\n<!-- agent: architect -->",
